#!/usr/bin/env bash
# promote-cards-by-token — on a release, move this repo's board cards to the
# "released" stage, correlating by the `card#<id>` token in the RELEASED RANGE's
# commit subjects.
#
# ⚑ VENDORED, NOT AUTHORED HERE. Everything BELOW THIS HEADER BLOCK is a BYTE-FOR-BYTE
# copy of `bin/promote-cards-by-token` in PupFuzz/agent-board-framework at commit
# e2f131f796baa93a5aa9cec620969bcaa21ac7fe (2026-07-27). Only this header was rewritten, and
# only to state MEZZANINE's facts instead of that repo's. Fix a defect UPSTREAM and re-vendor;
# a local edit to the body forks a security-sensitive tool away from the copy that has the
# review history, and nothing here would notice. The upstream repo is private, so the whole
# tool ships here rather than being fetched at run time — a public CI job cannot clone it.
#   RE-VENDOR = `sed -n '154,$p' <upstream>/bin/promote-cards-by-token > <this file's body>`,
#   keeping this header. That the body is unmodified is therefore a one-diff check, which is
#   the whole reason not one comment inside it was "tidied".
#   ⚑ READING THE BODY'S COMMENTS: every relative path they cite (`.githooks/…`,
#   `templates/…`, `plugins/…`) and every `card#NNNN` / `#NNN` they cite resolves in the
#   UPSTREAM repo — NOT in this one. Do not go looking for those files here. What plays their
#   role in mezzanine is named in this header: the grammar's in-repo consumer is
#   `bin/card-token-lint.py`, and the pin on this script's accept-set is
#   `bin/promote-cards-by-token.selftest.sh`.
#
# WHY THIS MOVER AND NOT THE TOOLKIT'S GENERIC ONE — the card-first correlation gap.
#   agent-board-toolkit's `promote-released-cards` correlates ONLY on `payload.dl_number` /
#   `payload.pr_number`. Board 14 is CARD-FIRST: cards are the front door, they carry no
#   `dl_number`, and this repo mints no DLs — so that mover has NO key to match on here and
#   its "Released to main" stage would be structurally unreachable. The webhook bridge's own
#   `merged_to_main` writeback leg is unreachable for the same shape: individual card PRs
#   squash-merge to `dev`, and the only PR that reaches `main` is the release PR, which names
#   no cards. The key that DOES exist is the `card#<id>` token already present in the
#   squash-commit subjects that `dev` carries onto `main`.
#
# CARD-TOKEN GRAMMAR — NOT OWNED HERE. The accept-set is owned by the webhook bridge's
# `GitHubPrCardMoveClassifier::CARD_TOKEN_PATTERN` (prose: agent-board-framework
# `plugins/coord/docs/BRIDGE-WRITEBACK.md` § The `card#<task-id>` convention, item 2). This
# comment explains the POSIX-ERE translation; it does not restate the grammar. Four properties
# are load-bearing: both separated aliases correlate (`card#123` and `card-123`); LEFT-anchored
# only, no trailing boundary (`card#3054_fix` still correlates); GLUED `card123` correlates at
# TWO OR MORE digits so that `card2go` still names no card; and near-miss shapes (`card_123`,
# `card:123`, `card #123`, single-digit glued `card4`) miss but never SILENTLY — they WARN.
# The bridge spells the two arms with a PCRE branch reset, `(?|[-#](\d+)|(\d{2,}))`, which
# POSIX ERE has no equivalent for; the two-arm form below is behaviourally identical because
# the arms are mutually exclusive on the character after `card`, so POSIX leftmost-LONGEST and
# PCRE leftmost-FIRST cannot disagree. IN THIS REPO the two patterns below are the single
# in-repo copy of that grammar: `bin/card-token-lint.py` EXTRACTS them from this file at run
# time rather than restating them, and `bin/promote-cards-by-token.selftest.sh` pins what this
# script's engine accepts. A widening happens upstream and arrives here by re-vendoring.
#
# SAFETY — the properties that make a terminal-stage mover trustworthy:
#   - POSITIVE-ONLY correlation. The card set is derived from commits REACHABLE in the
#     released range. Nothing is ever promoted because it is ABSENT from something.
#   - ANTI-RESURRECTION. A matched card whose CURRENT stage is not in the Shipped-class
#     source set is SKIPPED and logged, never moved (same semantics as
#     `promote-released-cards --shipped-stages`), so a Backlog / Won't-Do / already-
#     Released card carrying a matched token cannot be dragged into a TERMINAL stage.
#     Unlike the toolkit's, this guard is REQUIRED, not opt-in: this leg exists only
#     for a board whose released stage is terminal.
#   - BOARD-SCOPE. `card#<id>` resolves against a GLOBAL task-id space, so a token can
#     name a card on another board. A resolved card whose `board_id` is not the
#     configured board is REJECTED and reported loudly — the BRIDGE-WRITEBACK.md
#     § board-scope-guard invariant, applied to this mover.
#   - IDEMPOTENT. Re-running over the same range performs no writes and exits 0 (a re-run
#     over a NON-merge tip performs no writes and re-reports the same loud 4 — the tip does
#     not become trustworthy by being read twice).
#   - NEVER SILENT-GREEN. See the exit table below; every degraded state is loud.
#
# STRANDED-CARD RESIDUE REPORT — the NEGATIVE COMPLEMENT of the mover, and deliberately
# NOT a second promoter.
#   Positive-only correlation has one blind spot BY CONSTRUCTION: a card whose shipped commit
#   carried no parseable `card#<id>` token is INVISIBLE here — never matched, so never moved,
#   never counted, never mentioned. Exit 3 fires when a TOKEN names no card; there was no
#   signal at all for the inverse — a CARD that no token names. (Upstream MEASURED this on its
#   own board: ~8% of the released population had arrived by hand. No such measurement exists
#   for board 14 — this repo has never cut a release — so treat the rate here as UNKNOWN,
#   not as 8%.)
#   So after the moves, this reads the board and NAMES every card still sitting in a
#   Shipped-class stage that no token in the range named. Three rules keep it honest:
#     - IT NEVER PROMOTES. Every promotion decision stays positive-only; this leg performs no
#       write of any kind. "Absent from the range" is a REPORT, never a move.
#     - IT NEVER CHANGES THE EXIT CODE, in either direction — not for stranded cards, and not
#       for its own read failure. Stranded cards are a PRE-EXISTING BOARD CONDITION, not a
#       failure of this run: this run had no token for them and correctly did not move them,
#       and reddening the release job would neither fix one nor be actionable at that moment.
#       A red clearable only by editing history is a red that gets ignored — and it would
#       corrupt codes 1/3/4, which all mean "this run is degraded, act NOW". Loudness comes
#       from the register, not the code: the alarm is ALSO written to $GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY
#       under Actions, because a stderr line in a GREEN job is invisible in a collapsed log.
#     - IT NEVER PRINTS A CLEAN BILL IT DID NOT MEASURE. A blind read (0 visible cards), a
#       short read (pages deliver fewer rows than the board's own meta.total), a page-cap
#       overrun, an unparseable body or an HTTP error each report UNAVAILABLE — never
#       "0 stranded". The quiet path prints the census it actually took, so an empty result
#       is a measurement rather than a silence.
#   IT CANNOT CLASSIFY, and does not pretend to. A reported card is one of: (a) shipped here
#   under an untokened subject — it will never promote itself (closure: verify the commit,
#   then `--cards <id>`); (b) absorbed into a sibling card — same closure, and going forward
#   the absorbing commit should token EVERY absorbed card, which is what makes it reachable.
#   (A board-side `absorbed-by` link read by this tool is deliberately NOT the answer: it
#   would move the trust boundary from reviewed git history to a mutable card field, so
#   anyone with card-edit rights could point a card at a released sibling and drag it into a
#   terminal stage.) Or (c) merged to `dev` AFTER this release was cut — expected, and it
#   promotes on the NEXT release. Skipped entirely under `--cards` (a targeted promote has no
#   release range, so its complement is not residue) — loudly.
#   NB MALFORMED token spellings (`cards #N`, `card_N`, single-digit glued `card4`) are a
#   DIFFERENT gap — the near-miss WARN below, and `bin/card-token-lint.py`, which catches them
#   at PR time while renaming the branch is still free. This leg is the NO-TOKEN-AT-ALL case.
#
# EXIT CODES (distinguishable by design — a silent 0 is the failure this closes).
# Reported cumulatively (every degraded state prints), exiting on the MOST severe: 1 > 4 > 3.
# The stranded-card residue report is WARN-ONLY and never participates (see above).
#   0  healthy: every correlated token resolved, and each match either moved, was
#      already released, or was deliberately stage-guarded.
#   1  one or more card moves FAILED (non-2xx / transport error on a write). DIVERGENCE
#      from `promote-released-cards`, which exits 1 only when NOTHING moved — a green job
#      with one un-promoted shipped card. Here ANY failed write is non-zero.
#   2  refusal / fatal precondition, and NOTHING was written: bad config, missing
#      credentials, host-guard refusal, unreadable board, a missing option argument, or an
#      un-derivable range (no push-`before` and no release tag; or a `before` sha that is
#      absent from this checkout or not an ancestor of the head).
#   3  one or more card tokens matched NO card on the configured board (absent,
#      archived, or on another board). Deliberately NOT a warning: on this board every
#      `card#<id>` in a release range is supposed to be a live card here, so an
#      unresolved one means either a typo'd id (a card that will NEVER be promoted) or
#      an archived card — both need a human. A ~2-minute check is the right side of
#      the asymmetry against a silently un-promoted shipped card.
#   4  INCOMPLETE ref-derivation: the release tip is NOT a merge commit, so a squash/rebase
#      merge of the release PR may have collapsed the per-PR subjects this correlates on —
#      the silent-green class `promote-released-cards` also dies on. Whatever tokens the
#      range DID carry are still resolved and promoted FIRST: correlation is positive-only,
#      so a truncated range can only MISS cards, never promote a wrong one, and the moves it
#      can make are correct work worth keeping (a rebase-merged release preserves every
#      subject yet still has a non-merge tip). 4 then says "…and the set may be SHORT —
#      verify this release against the board by hand." Unlike 2, writes MAY have happened.
#      SECOND DIVERGENCE from `promote-released-cards`: this is checked on EVERY non-merge
#      tip, NEVER gated on the move count. Gating it on `moved == 0` (which this script
#      shipped with) lets a single surviving token suppress the whole guard — a tip that
#      collapsed 3 of 4 release subjects moves 1 card and exits 0 with 3 stranded forever.
#
# Usage: promote-cards-by-token [--cards LIST] [--base REF] [--head REF] [--dry-run] [--config PATH]
#   --cards "5101,card#5085"  promote exactly these (ids or tokens); skips git derivation.
#   --base REF                explicit range base; overrides the derivation below.
#   --head REF                range head (default HEAD).
#   --dry-run                 report only, move nothing.
#   --config PATH             config file (default .release-pr.json).
#
# Env: KANBAN_WRITEBACK_TOKEN (required)  — bearer token; its user must be a member of
#                                           the board WITH move permission.
#      KANBAN_EXPECTED_HOST   (required)  — the only host the token may be sent to.
#      KANBAN_API_BASE        (optional)  — overrides `.promote.api_base`; the committed
#                                           value is a host-scrubbed placeholder.
#      GITHUB_EVENT_BEFORE    (optional)  — the push event's `before` sha (see below).
#      GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY    (optional)  — set by GitHub Actions; the stranded-card alarm is
#                                           mirrored there so a WARN-only signal is visible on
#                                           a green job. Unwritable ⇒ ignored, never fatal.
set -euo pipefail

die() { echo "promote-cards-by-token: $*" >&2; exit 2; }
# warn_incomplete <lead> — REPORT the incomplete-derivation state (exit 4; see the table
# above). ONE message for ONE cause so the two call sites (empty token set / end of run)
# cannot drift. It only prints: the caller picks the exit code, so this can never preempt a
# more severe one (a failed write) the way an exit-inside-the-branch would.
warn_incomplete() {
  echo "promote-cards-by-token: FAILED — $1 the release tip ($HEAD_REF) is NOT a merge commit. A squash/rebase merge of the release PR collapses the per-PR subjects this correlates on, so any card whose token was dropped is silently un-promoted. Verify this release against the board by hand (promote the rest with --cards <ids>), and merge release PRs with a MERGE COMMIT (see the release-pr skill § Merge-button intent)." >&2
}
for c in jq curl git; do command -v "$c" >/dev/null || die "$c is required"; done

# in_shipped_stage <stage-id>: true iff the id is in the comma-separated $SHIPPED_STAGES set.
in_shipped_stage() { case ",$SHIPPED_STAGES," in *",$1,"*) return 0 ;; *) return 1 ;; esac; }

# host_ok <api_base>: true iff api_base is https:// and its host is $EXPECT_HOST or a
# subdomain of it. Host is parsed per RFC 3986 — the authority ends at the FIRST of
# '/', '?' or '#', then userinfo before the last '@' is stripped, then :port — so
# `https://good@evil/` (→ evil), `https://good.evil/`, and `https://evil#@good.host`
# are all rejected. The parser MUST agree with curl about where the authority ends.
# DELIBERATE MIRROR of `promote-released-cards`' host_ok (which is itself a mirror of
# the toolkit lib's kb_require_https_host, #3570): both are standalone tools that must
# not source a lib. Keep the copies in sync, INCLUDING the required-var check below.
# The in-repo pair is no longer kept in sync by THIS COMMENT ALONE: card#5127 bound them
# mechanically in `.githooks/promote-primitive-drift.selftest.py`, which drives both
# copies over a hostile-URL battery and fails naming the file that diverges — and which
# also fails when an UNREGISTERED third copy of the idiom appears anywhere in the tree.
# The toolkit's copy is cross-repo and stays comment-bound (card#5124 rule 5).
host_ok() {
  case "$1" in https://*) ;; *) return 1 ;; esac
  local h="${1#https://}"; h="${h%%[/?#]*}"; h="${h##*@}"; h="${h%%:*}"
  [ -n "$h" ] || return 1
  [ "$h" = "$EXPECT_HOST" ] && return 0
  case "$h" in *".$EXPECT_HOST") return 0 ;; *) return 1 ;; esac
}

# merge_commit_tip <ref>: true iff <ref> is a MERGE commit (>=2 parents). A merge-commit
# release tip means the range still carries every feature commit's subject, so the
# card-token derivation was COMPLETE; a non-merge tip (the release PR squash/rebase-
# merged) may have collapsed every per-PR subject into one `release: vX.Y.Z` line that
# names no card. git failure (unknown ref / shallow graft) ⇒ non-merge (fail-loud-safe);
# CI checks out fetch-depth:0, so parents are real.
merge_commit_tip() {
  local line
  line="$(git rev-list --parents -n1 "$1" 2>/dev/null)" || return 1
  [ "$(printf '%s' "$line" | wc -w)" -ge 3 ]
}

CONFIG=".release-pr.json"; CARDS_IN=""; BASE=""; HEAD_REF=""; DRY=0
# The value-taking options share ONE arity guard: without it `set -u` aborts on `$2` when
# such an option is the FINAL argument, and bash's `$2: unbound variable` exits 1 — the code
# this tool documents as "a card write FAILED". A malformed invocation is a precondition
# refusal (2), and it must be distinguishable from a half-completed release.
while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
  case "$1" in
    --cards|--base|--head|--config)
      [ $# -ge 2 ] || die "option '$1' requires an argument. Usage: promote-cards-by-token [--cards LIST] [--base REF] [--head REF] [--dry-run] [--config PATH]"
      case "$1" in
        --cards)  CARDS_IN="$2";;
        --base)   BASE="$2";;
        --head)   HEAD_REF="$2";;
        --config) CONFIG="$2";;
      esac
      shift 2;;
    --dry-run) DRY=1; shift;;
    # Print the header block and STOP at the first non-comment line. Deliberately NOT a
    # fixed line range, which gets this wrong in BOTH directions as the file moves: the
    # previous `sed -n '2,90p'` leaked `set -euo pipefail` and three helpers into user-facing
    # output when the header was 82 lines, and would silently truncate the Env: block now
    # that it is longer. Pinned by the selftest as a line-count equality, not an excerpt.
    -h|--help) awk 'NR>1 { if (substr($0,1,1) == "#") print; else exit }' "$0"; exit 0;;
    *) die "unknown arg '$1'";;
  esac
done

# SECURITY: .release-pr.json is security-sensitive — `.promote.api_base` is where the
# writeback token is sent. A PR editing it to an attacker host would exfiltrate
# KANBAN_WRITEBACK_TOKEN on the next release run. Treat ANY api_base change in a PR as a
# CREDENTIAL-SCOPE change and review it as one. Defense: the committed value is a
# host-scrubbed placeholder, the real base arrives via the repo VARIABLE KANBAN_API_BASE
# (out-of-band from this PR-editable file), and the host guard below fail-closes on
# $KANBAN_EXPECTED_HOST (also a repo variable) BEFORE any token leaves the runner.
[ -f "$CONFIG" ] || die "no $CONFIG in this repo"
jq -e . "$CONFIG" >/dev/null 2>&1 || die "$CONFIG is not valid JSON — fix it (the release-pr skill and the #225 tag-prefix guard read it too)"
cfg() { jq -r "$1 // empty" "$CONFIG"; }
BOARD="$(cfg '.promote.board_id')"
STAGE="$(cfg '.promote.released_stage_id')"
SHIPPED_STAGES="$(cfg '.promote.shipped_stage_ids')"
TAG_FORMAT="$(cfg '.tag_format')"
[ -n "$TAG_FORMAT" ] || TAG_FORMAT='v{{version}}'
API="${KANBAN_API_BASE:-}"; [ -n "$API" ] || API="$(cfg '.promote.api_base')"
[ -n "$BOARD" ] && [ -n "$STAGE" ] && [ -n "$SHIPPED_STAGES" ] && [ -n "$API" ] \
  || die ".promote.{board_id,released_stage_id,shipped_stage_ids,api_base} are all required in $CONFIG"
case "$BOARD" in ''|*[!0-9]*) die ".promote.board_id must be numeric (got '$BOARD')";; esac
case "$STAGE" in ''|*[!0-9]*) die ".promote.released_stage_id must be numeric (got '$STAGE')";; esac
# The Shipped-class source set is REQUIRED here (see ANTI-RESURRECTION above). A
# malformed set is a config error, not a silent no-guard — fail loud.
SHIPPED_STAGES="$(printf '%s' "$SHIPPED_STAGES" | tr -d '[:space:]')"
case "$SHIPPED_STAGES" in
  ''|*[!0-9,]*|,*|*,|*,,*) die ".promote.shipped_stage_ids must be a comma-separated list of numeric stage ids (got '$SHIPPED_STAGES')";;
esac
in_shipped_stage "$STAGE" && die ".promote.released_stage_id ($STAGE) is also listed in shipped_stage_ids — the released stage can never be its own source; fix $CONFIG"
API="${API%/}"

TOKEN="${KANBAN_WRITEBACK_TOKEN:-}"
[ -n "$TOKEN" ] || die "KANBAN_WRITEBACK_TOKEN is not set — set the repo Actions secret (a kanban token whose user is a member of board $BOARD with move permission)"
# Fail-closed host guard BEFORE any token is sent. The expected host is REQUIRED with NO
# baked default: this repo's board lives on an operator-run kanban, so there is no host
# safe to assume. Pin it as a repo/org VARIABLE — out-of-band from the PR-editable
# $CONFIG, so an attacker's api_base edit cannot also relax the constraint.
EXPECT_HOST="${KANBAN_EXPECTED_HOST:-}"
[ -n "$EXPECT_HOST" ] \
  || die "KANBAN_EXPECTED_HOST must be set to the expected api host before sending the writeback token; refusing to send (set the repo variable KANBAN_EXPECTED_HOST)"
host_ok "$API" \
  || die "refusing to send token — api_base '$API' is not https:// on '$EXPECT_HOST' (or a subdomain). Set the repo variable KANBAN_API_BASE to the real base; the committed $CONFIG value is a host-scrubbed placeholder."

# --- derive the shipped card ids ---------------------------------------------
# The shared card-token grammar (see the header). `grep -o` naturally stops at the first
# non-digit, which IS the "left-anchored, no trailing boundary" property.
# Bound to the in-repo authority `_CARD_RE` in kanban-prs-sync.py by
# .githooks/promote-primitive-drift.selftest.py (card#5127): it EXTRACTS both patterns at run
# time and compares what each ENGINE accepts (a textual diff is impossible across a PCRE
# lookahead + re.I and a POSIX-ERE alternation + `grep -i`). Pinned here rather than extracted
# at run time the way .githooks/card-token-lint.py does — see that guard's docstring for the
# reasoning.
CARD_RE='\bcard([-#][0-9]+|[0-9]{2,})'
# The near-miss probe from the same spec, in POSIX ERE (`\s` → `[[:space:]]`).
NEAR_MISS_RE='\bcard([_:.]|[[:space:]]#)?[0-9]'
# numlist: bare integers, leading zeros stripped (keeping >=1 digit), deduped, order-preserving.
numlist() { grep -oE '[0-9]+' | sed 's/^0*\([0-9]\)/\1/' | awk 'NF && !seen[$0]++'; }

RANGE=""
if [ -n "$CARDS_IN" ]; then
  # Explicit input: accept both spellings (`5101` and `card#5101`) — an operator typing
  # a dispatch input writes bare ids, a copy/paste carries tokens.
  IDS="$(printf '%s' "$CARDS_IN" | numlist || true)"
  [ -n "$IDS" ] || die "--cards '$CARDS_IN' contains no card ids"
  DERIVE_COMPLETE=1
  RANGE="--cards '$CARDS_IN'"
else
  HEAD_REF="${HEAD_REF:-HEAD}"
  # RANGE BASE — three sources, most-authoritative first. Rationale in the PR body:
  #  1. $GITHUB_EVENT_BEFORE (push payload `before`) is the sha `main` pointed at BEFORE
  #     this push. It is MEASURED, not inferred, so `before..after` is exactly the set of
  #     commits this push added — the definition of "newly released". It is also stable
  #     across a re-run of the same job (the payload is fixed), which is what makes the
  #     idempotent re-run re-scan the SAME range.
  #  2. The previous release TAG reachable from ${HEAD_REF}^ — the fallback for
  #     workflow_dispatch, which carries no push payload. `^` (not HEAD) so the tag the
  #     auto-tagger just minted ON this release merge does not collapse the range to empty.
  #  3. Nothing → REFUSE. NB the failure of an empty base is a SILENT NO-OP, not the
  #     mass-move `promote-released-cards`' comment claims: git resolves an omitted left
  #     side to HEAD, so `..HEAD` is `HEAD..HEAD` = EMPTY (verified: `git log --oneline
  #     ..HEAD` on this repo yields 0 commits against 647 for `git log HEAD`). Falling
  #     through would therefore print "nothing to do" and exit 0 on a first-ever release
  #     whose cards all genuinely shipped — a false all-clear, the exact class this tool
  #     exists to close. Either way the answer is REFUSE; the escape is --base / --cards.
  #     (The toolkit comment's mass-move rationale is being reported to its owner.)
  # Deliberately NOT used: the merge's first parent (`HEAD^1`). It is only defined when
  # the tip IS a merge, it silently narrows to a single commit on a squash tip (the exact
  # silent-green this tool must not have), and it drops any commit pushed to main between
  # releases — all three of which (1) gets right for free.
  if [ -z "$BASE" ]; then
    gb="${GITHUB_EVENT_BEFORE:-}"
    case "$gb" in ''|*[!0-9a-fA-F]*) gb="" ;; esac   # absent / not a hex sha
    case "$gb" in *[!0]*) ;; *) gb="" ;; esac        # all-zero sha = branch creation
    if [ -n "$gb" ]; then
      git rev-parse --verify --quiet "${gb}^{commit}" >/dev/null \
        || die "push-event base '$gb' is not a commit in this checkout (a force-push whose old tip was never fetched, or a shallow clone without fetch-depth:0). Refusing to guess a range — re-run with an explicit --base."
      # EXISTENCE is not ENOUGH. `before..head` is "what this push added" only when `before`
      # is an ANCESTOR of the head. After a force-push that rewrote the branch, the old tip
      # can still be PRESENT in the checkout (reachable from a tag or another branch) yet sit
      # on a DIVERGED line, and `before..head` then spans the rewrite and re-scans commits
      # released long ago. Positive-only correlation + the stage guard mean such a range
      # cannot promote a card that never shipped, so this is a measurement-integrity check,
      # not a containment one — but it is the check the header and the PR body claim, and a
      # claim the code does not make true is the defect either way.
      git merge-base --is-ancestor "$gb" "$HEAD_REF" \
        || die "push-event base '$gb' exists but is NOT an ancestor of $HEAD_REF — the branch was force-pushed/rewritten, so '$gb..$HEAD_REF' is not the set this push added. Refusing to guess a range — re-run with an explicit --base."
      BASE="$gb"
    fi
  fi
  if [ -z "$BASE" ]; then
    case "$TAG_FORMAT" in
      *'{{version}}') TAG_GLOB="${TAG_FORMAT%'{{version}}'}" ;;
      *) die "tag_format '$TAG_FORMAT' does not end with {{version}} — cannot derive the release-tag glob (same rule as the #225 tag-prefix guard)";;
    esac
    BASE="$(git describe --tags --abbrev=0 --match "${TAG_GLOB}*" "${HEAD_REF}^" 2>/dev/null || true)"
  fi
  [ -n "$BASE" ] \
    || die "no range base — no push-event 'before' sha and no release tag before $HEAD_REF (a first-ever release, or a shallow/tagless clone). With no base the range would be EMPTY and this run would report a false all-clear ('nothing to do') while every genuinely-shipped card stayed un-promoted. Re-run with an explicit --base <ref> or --cards <ids>."
  RANGE="$BASE..$HEAD_REF"
  SUBJECTS="$(git log "$RANGE" --format='%s' 2>/dev/null)" \
    || die "git log $RANGE failed — bad base/head ref"
  IDS="$(printf '%s\n' "$SUBJECTS" | grep -oiE "$CARD_RE" | numlist || true)"
  merge_commit_tip "$HEAD_REF" && DERIVE_COMPLETE=1 || DERIVE_COMPLETE=0
  # Near-miss WARN (DL-201 property 3): a subject whose token LOOKS like a card ref but
  # does not correlate is an author's silent miss — surface it. Warn-only, matching the
  # bridge's posture; a near-miss is not evidence that anything shipped.
  while IFS= read -r line; do
    [ -n "$line" ] || continue
    echo "promote-cards-by-token: WARNING — near-miss card token in a released commit subject (does NOT correlate; use card#<id>, card-<id>, or glued card<id> with TWO OR MORE digits — single-digit glued 'card4' does not parse): $line" >&2
  done <<< "$(printf '%s\n' "$SUBJECTS" | grep -iE "$NEAR_MISS_RE" | grep -viE "$CARD_RE" || true)"
fi

# --- api seam ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Set up BEFORE the empty-token exits below: those paths still run the stranded-card
# residue report, and an EMPTY token set is the case where the residue is MAXIMAL (a range
# that named nothing left EVERYTHING behind). Exiting there without a board read was the
# one place this tool could print "nothing to do", exit 0, and be wrong about the board.
TMPD="$(mktemp -d)"; trap 'rm -rf "$TMPD"' EXIT
BODY="$TMPD/resp.json"

# api <METHOD> <url> [json-body] — perform the call, leave the response body at $BODY,
# print the HTTP status code ('000' on a transport failure).
# Auth rides a stdin herestring (-H @-) so the bearer token never appears in curl's argv
# (world-readable via `ps aux` on a multi-user host, #3569). --retry backs off on a
# TRANSIENT error only (408/429/5xx/timeout/refused) — never a 4xx — so a real 401/404
# still fails fast; card moves are idempotent, so retrying a PATCH is safe.
api() {
  local method="$1" url="$2" data="${3:-}" code
  local -a args=(-sS --retry 5 --retry-connrefused --retry-max-time 60
                 -H @- -H "Accept: application/json"
                 -o "$BODY" -w '%{http_code}' -X "$method")
  [ -n "$data" ] && args+=(-H "Content-Type: application/json" --data-binary "$data")
  args+=("$url")
  : > "$BODY"
  code="$(curl "${args[@]}" <<<"$(printf 'Authorization: Bearer %s' "$TOKEN")" || true)"
  printf '%s' "${code:-000}"
}

# --- stranded-card residue report (see the header section of the same name) --------------
# BOARD-READ-ONLY, WARN-ONLY, and never part of the exit policy. Nothing below issues a
# non-GET board call or sets a variable the exit policy reads (its only write of any kind is
# appending to $GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY) — that separation is the whole safety argument for adding
# a negative-side signal to a positive-only mover.
#
# THE ONE RESIDUE IT CANNOT SEE, stated because a report's blind spots are part of its
# contract: a card the TOKEN'S USER cannot see. A per-card visibility restriction hides the
# row AND shrinks meta.total in step with it, so neither the blind-read guard (which catches
# only total invisibility) nor the short-read census can detect a partial one. Board
# membership is the backstop, and it is checked by the preflight on the promoting path only.
RESIDUE_PAGE_CAP=50

# residue_alarm <text> — one body, two sinks. stderr always; the Actions job summary too when
# $GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY is set, because this report is deliberately WARN-only and a stderr line
# in a GREEN job is effectively invisible in a collapsed step log. Single-sourced so the two
# surfaces cannot drift, and never fatal: an unwritable summary path must not fail a release.
residue_alarm() {
  printf '%s\n' "$1" >&2
  [ -n "${GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY:-}" ] || return 0
  { printf '### promote-cards-by-token — stranded-card report\n\n```\n%s\n```\n' "$1" \
      >> "$GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY"; } 2>/dev/null || true
}

# residue_unavailable <why> — the residue could not be MEASURED. Loud, because "nothing was
# left behind" and "I could not tell" are the same silence otherwise (the 200-but-empty class,
# RELEASE-AUTOMATION.md § Robustness patterns).
residue_unavailable() {
  residue_alarm "promote-cards-by-token: WARNING — stranded-card report UNAVAILABLE ($1). Do NOT read this run as 'nothing was left behind': the residue was NOT measured. (Report-only — this does not change the exit code.)"
}

# residue_report — page the WHOLE board, census the read, and name every LIVE card of THIS
# board sitting in a Shipped-class stage whose id no token in $IDS named. Never dies (a
# `return` on every degraded path), never writes, never touches the exit code.
residue_report() {
  local page=1 code data n total='' last_page='' sum_n=0 read_n cards='[]'
  local named shipped shipped_n rows rows_n
  while :; do
    code="$(api GET "$API/tasks/search.json?q=board_id=$BOARD&limit=200&page=$page")"
    [ "$code" = "200" ] || { residue_unavailable "board $BOARD page $page read returned HTTP $code against $API"; return 0; }
    data="$(jq -c '.data // []' < "$BODY" 2>/dev/null || true)"
    [ -n "$data" ] || { residue_unavailable "board $BOARD page $page returned an unparseable body"; return 0; }
    n="$(printf '%s' "$data" | jq 'length' 2>/dev/null || echo 0)"
    if [ "$page" -eq 1 ]; then
      # Same semantics as the toolkit's fetch_whole_board: meta.last_page is a SECONDARY
      # termination signal, usable only as a POSITIVE integer — the n<200 short-page break is
      # the primary one, so an absent/0/garbage last_page must fall through to it rather than
      # break the scan at page 1 on a full page (a silent truncation).
      total="$(jq -r '.meta.total // empty' < "$BODY" 2>/dev/null || true)"
      last_page="$(jq -r '.meta.last_page // empty' < "$BODY" 2>/dev/null || true)"
      case "$total" in ''|*[!0-9]*) total='' ;; esac
      case "$last_page" in ''|*[!0-9]*) last_page='' ;; esac
      [ -n "$last_page" ] && [ "$last_page" -lt 1 ] && last_page=''
    fi
    # Concatenate via STDIN, never --argjson: a board page can exceed MAX_ARG_STRLEN (~128KB).
    cards="$(printf '%s\n%s\n' "$cards" "$data" | jq -c -s 'add' 2>/dev/null || true)"
    [ -n "$cards" ] || { residue_unavailable "board $BOARD page $page could not be merged into the card set"; return 0; }
    sum_n=$((sum_n + n))
    [ "$n" -lt 200 ] && break
    [ -n "$last_page" ] && [ "$page" -ge "$last_page" ] && break
    page=$((page + 1))
    if [ "$page" -gt "$RESIDUE_PAGE_CAP" ]; then
      residue_unavailable "board $BOARD exceeded the ${RESIDUE_PAGE_CAP}-page read cap — the read is TRUNCATED, so an unnamed card could sit past the cut"
      return 0
    fi
  done
  # Dedup by id — an API that ignores `page=` (same page each loop) or returns overlapping
  # pages would otherwise inflate the residue with duplicates.
  cards="$(printf '%s' "$cards" | jq -c 'unique_by(.id)')"
  read_n="$(printf '%s' "$cards" | jq 'length')"
  if [ "${read_n:-0}" -eq 0 ]; then
    residue_unavailable "board $BOARD returned 0 visible cards — a blind read (the token's user is likely not a member of board $BOARD, or this is the wrong instance); refusing to conclude that nothing was left behind"
    return 0
  fi
  # Short-read census: the server delivered fewer rows than it says exist ⇒ an unnamed card
  # could be in the part that was never read ⇒ report UNAVAILABLE, never "0 stranded".
  # sum_n >= total with read_n < total is a page-boundary dedup artifact — the read IS
  # complete (same discrimination as the toolkit's fetch_whole_board).
  if [ -n "$total" ] && [ "$total" -gt "${read_n:-0}" ] && [ "$sum_n" -lt "$total" ]; then
    residue_unavailable "board $BOARD reports $total cards but the pages delivered only $sum_n ($read_n distinct) — an INCOMPLETE read"
    return 0
  fi
  # $IDS is the set THIS RUN's range named — the exclusion is by NAMED-set, not by current
  # stage, so a card whose move failed (already loud, exit 1) is not double-reported here, and
  # a --dry-run's un-moved matches are not reported as stranded either.
  named="$(printf '%s\n' "$IDS" | jq -Rsc 'split("\n") | map(select(length > 0))')"
  # "Shipped-class, live, on THIS board" — defined ONCE, so the census and the residue list
  # can never diverge. The board_id re-check is boundary validation of the search response,
  # not paranoia: the query is the only thing scoping it, and a server that ignored `q` would
  # otherwise let another board's cards be reported as this board's residue.
  shipped="$(printf '%s' "$cards" | jq -c --arg bd "$BOARD" --arg stages ",$SHIPPED_STAGES," '
    [ .[]
      | select((.board_id | tostring) == $bd)
      | select(((.archived_at // .deleted_at) // null) == null)
      | select((.workflow_stage_id | tostring) as $s | $stages | contains("," + $s + ",")) ]')"
  shipped_n="$(printf '%s' "$shipped" | jq 'length')"
  rows="$(printf '%s' "$shipped" | jq -r --argjson named "$named" '
    [ .[] | select((.id | tostring) as $i | ($named | index($i)) == null) ]
    | sort_by(.id)
    | .[]
    | "  ! card#\(.id) (stage \(.workflow_stage_id))"
      + (if ((.title // "") | tostring | length) > 0 then " — \(.title)" else "" end)')"
  if [ -z "$rows" ]; then
    echo "promote-cards-by-token: stranded-card report — 0 unnamed: all $shipped_n Shipped-class card(s) (stages $SHIPPED_STAGES) on board $BOARD were named by $RANGE (board read: $read_n card(s))."
    return 0
  fi
  rows_n="$(printf '%s\n' "$rows" | grep -c . || true)"
  # The list is NEVER truncated and is sorted by id: the residue is meant to be a small,
  # stable, diffable set that shrinks as it is closed out. Truncating it would re-create the
  # exact "everything past the cut doesn't exist" miss this report exists to close.
  residue_alarm "$(printf '%s\n%s\n%s' \
    "promote-cards-by-token: WARNING — STRANDED-CARD REPORT: $rows_n of $shipped_n Shipped-class card(s) (stages $SHIPPED_STAGES) on board $BOARD were named by NO card#<id> token in $RANGE:" \
    "$rows" \
    "  Each is one of: (a) work that shipped under a commit subject carrying no parseable card# token — it will NEVER promote itself (verify the commit, then promote by hand: --cards <ids>); (b) work absorbed into a sibling card whose commit tokens only the sibling — same closure, and token every absorbed card in the absorbing commit from now on; or (c) a card merged to the integration branch AFTER this release was cut — expected, it promotes on the next release. This report cannot tell them apart, promotes nothing, and does NOT change this run's exit code.")"
}

# maybe_residue_report — the ONE call-shape both call sites use, so the --cards carve-out
# cannot be applied at one site and forgotten at the other.
maybe_residue_report() {
  if [ -n "$CARDS_IN" ]; then
    echo "promote-cards-by-token: stranded-card report SKIPPED — --cards is a targeted promote, not a release; the Shipped-class residue is not this run's complement."
    return 0
  fi
  residue_report
}

if [ -z "$IDS" ]; then
  # An empty token set is the MAXIMAL-residue case, so the report runs here too — before the
  # incomplete-derivation FAILED line, which stays the last thing an operator reads.
  if [ "$DERIVE_COMPLETE" = 1 ]; then
    echo "promote-cards-by-token: no card#<id> tokens in $RANGE — nothing to do."
    maybe_residue_report
    exit 0
  fi
  maybe_residue_report
  warn_incomplete "no card#<id> tokens in $RANGE ('no tokens' here is indistinguishable from 'tokens were destroyed') and"
  exit 4
fi

# PREFLIGHT — one board read whose ONLY job is to tell "the token cannot see this board"
# apart from "no card matched". Without it, a token that lost board membership would make
# every per-id read 404 and the run would look like "all cards archived" instead of
# "credentials are wrong" (200-but-empty is the classic silent-green — see
# RELEASE-AUTOMATION.md § Robustness patterns). This is also why this tool does NOT need
# the toolkit's whole-board paging + short-read census: its correlation key is the card's
# own id, so an unreadable or incomplete board announces itself per token (exit 3) rather
# than hiding as an absent payload stamp.
code="$(api GET "$API/tasks/search.json?q=board_id=$BOARD&limit=1")"
[ "$code" = "200" ] \
  || die "board $BOARD preflight read failed (HTTP $code) against $API — check KANBAN_WRITEBACK_TOKEN and the api base."
nvis="$(jq '(.data // []) | length' < "$BODY" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)"
[ "${nvis:-0}" -gt 0 ] \
  || die "board $BOARD returned 0 visible cards — the token's user is likely not a member of board $BOARD (or this is the wrong kanban instance). Refusing to conclude 'nothing to promote' from a blind read."

# --- resolve + move ----------------------------------------------------------
moved=0; already=0; guarded=0; unmatched=0; failed=0
for id in $IDS; do
  code="$(api GET "$API/tasks/$id.json")"
  case "$code" in
    200) ;;
    404) echo "  ? card#$id: no such card — absent or deleted (report: it may have been archived, or the token is a typo)" >&2
         unmatched=$((unmatched + 1)); continue ;;
    *)   die "card#$id read failed (HTTP $code) — refusing to continue on a degraded board read (a partial run would leave shipped cards silently un-promoted)." ;;
  esac
  cbd="$(jq -r '.data.board_id // empty' < "$BODY")"
  cur="$(jq -r '.data.workflow_stage_id // empty' < "$BODY")"
  gone="$(jq -r '(.data.archived_at // .data.deleted_at) // empty' < "$BODY")"
  # BOARD-SCOPE guard (BRIDGE-WRITEBACK.md § Board-scope guard on the `card#` fallback):
  # task-ids are global, so a token can name another board's card. Reject loudly; never
  # let commit text redirect a move across boards.
  if [ "$cbd" != "$BOARD" ]; then
    echo "  ? card#$id: on board $cbd, not board $BOARD — REJECTED (a card token must not move an out-of-board card)" >&2
    unmatched=$((unmatched + 1)); continue
  fi
  if [ -n "$gone" ]; then
    echo "  ? card#$id: archived/deleted ($gone) — not on the board; left alone" >&2
    unmatched=$((unmatched + 1)); continue
  fi
  if [ "$cur" = "$STAGE" ]; then
    echo "  = card#$id: already released (stage $STAGE)"; already=$((already + 1)); continue
  fi
  # ANTI-RESURRECTION. Evaluated AFTER the already-released check so an idempotent re-run
  # of a genuinely-released card reads as "already released", not as a stage-guarded skip.
  if ! in_shipped_stage "$cur"; then
    echo "  ⊘ card#$id: current stage $cur is not a Shipped-class source stage ($SHIPPED_STAGES) — SKIPPED; promotion never resurrects a backlog/declined card into a terminal stage" >&2
    guarded=$((guarded + 1)); continue
  fi
  if [ "$DRY" = 1 ]; then
    echo "  → card#$id: would move $cur → $STAGE"; moved=$((moved + 1)); continue
  fi
  # FLAT top-level write body — never a `{"task": {...}}` resource wrapper (kanban
  # v0.36.0 / DL-219 strict-rejects it). Ratified flat write contract, roundtable #119;
  # this is the bash authority's shape and the selftest pins it.
  code="$(api PATCH "$API/tasks/$id.json" "$(jq -nc --argjson s "$STAGE" '{workflow_stage_id: $s}')")"
  case "$code" in
    2??) echo "  ✓ card#$id: moved $cur → $STAGE"; moved=$((moved + 1)) ;;
    *)   echo "  ✗ card#$id: move failed (HTTP $code) — left in place" >&2; failed=$((failed + 1)) ;;
  esac
done

echo "promote-cards-by-token: $moved moved, $already already-released, $guarded stage-guarded, $unmatched no-card, $failed failed$([ "$DRY" = 1 ] && printf ' (dry-run)')."

# The negative complement of everything above — read-only, and evaluated BEFORE the exit
# policy so the FAILED lines that DO carry a code stay the last thing on stderr.
maybe_residue_report

# --- exit policy (see the EXIT CODES table in the header) --------------------
# EVERY degraded state PRINTS; the exit code is chosen afterwards, most-severe-first.
# Exiting inside the first matching branch is the same defect shape this section fixes at the
# guard level — it lets whichever condition is checked first suppress the others' signal, and
# an operator reading a red job needs ALL of them, not the one that happened to win.
#
# A write failure is ALWAYS non-zero, even when other cards moved: a green job with one
# un-promoted shipped card is the silent-green class this tool exists to close.
# (`promote-released-cards` exits 1 only when NOTHING moved; that partial-failure hole is
# being raised with the toolkit owner.)
if [ "$failed" -gt 0 ]; then
  echo "promote-cards-by-token: FAILED — $failed card move(s) errored; those cards are still un-promoted." >&2
fi
# Newly promoted NOTHING despite a non-empty token set, from a COMPLETE derivation
# (explicit --cards, or a MERGE-commit release tip whose range carries every feature
# subject) ⇒ the tokens are genuinely already-released / stage-guarded ⇒ WARN, exit 0.
# The idempotent re-run lands here — and must stay quiet-and-0, so this is the ONLY
# thing keyed on the move count.
if [ "$moved" = 0 ] && [ "$DERIVE_COMPLETE" = 1 ]; then
  echo "promote-cards-by-token: WARNING — 0 cards newly promoted (all matches were already released or stage-guarded)." >&2
fi
if [ "$unmatched" -gt 0 ]; then
  echo "promote-cards-by-token: FAILED — $unmatched card token(s) matched no card on board $BOARD (absent, archived, or on another board). Each is a card that will never be promoted; resolve them by hand." >&2
fi
# INCOMPLETE DERIVATION — evaluated on EVERY non-merge tip, INDEPENDENT of how many cards
# moved. This was previously nested inside `if moved == 0`, which meant one surviving token
# suppressed the guard entirely: a squash tip that kept 1 of 4 subjects moved 1 card and
# exited 0, stranding the other 3 with no signal at all.
if [ "$DERIVE_COMPLETE" != 1 ]; then
  warn_incomplete "$moved card(s) were promoted from $RANGE, but"
fi

# Exit on the MOST SEVERE state reported above: 1 > 4 > 3. A failed write is a KNOWN
# stranded card; 4 says the token SET ITSELF may be short, which subsumes 3's per-token miss.
# The stranded-card residue report is deliberately ABSENT from this ladder (header § Stranded-
# card residue report): it describes the BOARD's history, not this run's integrity, and
# smuggling it into 1/3/4 would blunt three codes that mean "act now".
if [ "$failed" -gt 0 ];        then exit 1; fi
if [ "$DERIVE_COMPLETE" != 1 ]; then exit 4; fi
if [ "$unmatched" -gt 0 ];     then exit 3; fi
exit 0
